 \paragraph{Finite origins, distant origins, and restricted repair.}
-Projected classifiers study fixed views \citep{Hamsici2007,Hamsici2008,Oh2025}. Radial-projection geometry studies how those views change as the origin moves \citep{Orponen2019}. Geometric wedge-separation research supplies a basis for reasoning about obstructed angular separability \citep{Hurtado2001}. Positive loss at each finite origin by itself does not imply a positive \emph{global} infimum: the loss could approach zero as the origin escapes to infinity. We resolve that boundary through convergence of Bayes risk to orthogonal-projection risk. Finally, decision-aware quantization provides precedent for preserving labels with compressed inputs \citep{OehlerGray1995,Senozan2024}; our repair establishes that one common radial bit, combined with direction, preserves the decision throughout a specified continuous class support.
